MODULE 1 — CONTINENTAL LEGAL FRAMEWORKS & PAN-AFRICAN REGULATORY HARMONIZATION
Duration: 1.5 hours
1.1 Module Overview
Artificial intelligence does not operate in a legal vacuum.

An AI system may simultaneously process:
- personal information;
- biometric information;
- financial data;
- health information;
- educational records;
- employment information;
- government information;
- commercially sensitive data;
- cross-border data.
Consequently, an African AI deployment may be subject to several layers of regulation.

Think of the regulatory environment as a hierarchy:

Continental frameworks → Regional frameworks → National legislation → Sector regulation → Institutional policies → Technical controls

The objective is not simply to ask:
"Is AI legal?"

Instead, ask:
"What data is being processed, where is it processed, for what purpose, by whom, under whose jurisdiction, and with what safeguards?"

1.2 The African Regulatory Landscape
Africa does not have one single AI or data-protection law.

Instead, governance operates through multiple overlapping frameworks.

Important continental instruments include:
African Union Convention on Cyber Security and Personal Data Protection
Commonly called the Malabo Convention, it was adopted in 2014 and entered into force on 8 June 2023. African Union

The Convention addresses areas including:
- personal data protection;
- cybersecurity;
- electronic transactions;
- cybercrime;
- institutional cooperation.
It is important because it establishes a continental reference point for cybersecurity and personal-data governance.

However:
The Malabo Convention does not replace national data-protection laws.

Students must therefore avoid treating it as an "African GDPR."
1.3 AU Data Policy Framework
The African Union adopted its Data Policy Framework in 2022.

The framework aims to strengthen and harmonize African data-governance frameworks and promote a trusted African data environment, including support for intra-African digital trade and AfCFTA-related integration. African Union

The framework emphasizes:
- data governance;
- data rights;
- trusted data ecosystems;
- cross-border data flows;
- digital transformation;
- economic development;
- inclusion;
- African data infrastructure.
Key principle
Data should not merely be viewed as a technical asset.

It is simultaneously:

an economic resource + a governance resource + a rights-bearing resource + a strategic resource.
1.4 National Regulatory Layer
A company deploying an AI platform across Africa must still examine the law of each relevant jurisdiction.

Examples include:
South Africa
South Africa's Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA) establishes requirements governing the processing of personal information.
Kenya
Kenya's Data Protection Act, 2019 regulates personal-data processing and establishes the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner. Kenya also has regulations addressing registration, complaints, enforcement and other areas. odpc.go.ke

Kenya's framework contains specific requirements for transferring personal data outside Kenya. odpc.go.ke
Nigeria
Nigeria's Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 establishes a legal framework for protecting personal information and establishes the Nigeria Data Protection Commission. The Act can apply to processing connected to Nigeria even where an organization is not physically domiciled there. ndpc.gov.ng
