MODULE 3 — DATA SOVEREIGNTY, SOVEREIGN CLOUD & LOCAL RESIDENCY ARCHITECTURES
Duration: 1.5 hours
3.1 Module Overview
Modern AI creates a major architectural problem.

Organizations want access to:
- frontier AI models;
- global cloud infrastructure;
- advanced GPUs;
- global developer platforms;
- international APIs.
At the same time, governments and institutions may require:
- local data residency;
- national control;
- restricted cross-border transfers;
- sovereign infrastructure;
- stronger security;
- institutional ownership.
The answer is not necessarily:
"Never use international cloud."

Instead, organizations can design controlled hybrid architectures.
3.2 Four Concepts Students Must Distinguish
Data Residency
Where the data is physically stored or processed.

Example:
"Customer data must remain in South Africa."

Data Sovereignty
The legal and governmental authority applicable to data.

Example:

Data may be physically located in one country but subject to another jurisdiction's legal control depending on the architecture and contractual structure.
Data Localization
A legal or policy requirement that particular categories of data remain within a specified jurisdiction.
Data Control
Who has practical authority over:
- access;
- encryption;
- processing;
- deletion;
- movement;
- permissions.
3.3 The Sovereign AI Principle
A useful design principle is:
Sensitive data should remain under institutional control even when frontier intelligence is accessed externally.

This creates an architectural separation between:
Data Plane
Where sensitive information resides.

and
Intelligence Plane
Where AI reasoning/model capabilities are accessed.
